Set theory as the foundation of data types. Every data structure I build is an implementation of a mathematical set, a relation, or a function — and understanding the mathematical object clarifies what operations the data structure must support.

A set is a collection of distinct elements with no ordering. The fundamental operations: membership test (x in S?), union (S ∪ T), intersection (S ∩ T), difference (S \ T), subset test (S ⊆ T?).

A relation R on sets A and B is a subset of A × B — a set of ordered pairs. A function f: A → B is a relation where each element of A appears in exactly one pair. A dictionary (hash map) is an implementation of a partial function: not every key needs to have a value.

The mathematical properties directly determine algorithmic requirements:
  - Sets have no duplicates → insert must check for membership first
  - Relations are sets of pairs → we can index by either component
  - Functions map each input to exactly one output → update overwrites, does not append
  - Equivalence relations (reflexive, symmetric, transitive) → naturally partition a set into classes → Union-Find data structure

Let me implement sets from scratch to show how the mathematical definition drives the implementation:

class OrderedSet:
    """Set implemented as a sorted array.
    Membership: O(log n) via binary search.
    Insert: O(n) due to shifting.
    Union/Intersection: O(n + m) via merge."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.data = []

    def _bisect(self, x):
        lo, hi = 0, len(self.data)
        while lo < hi:
            mid = (lo + hi) // 2
            if self.data[mid] < x:
                lo = mid + 1
            else:
                hi = mid
        return lo

    def contains(self, x):
        i = self._bisect(x)
        return i < len(self.data) and self.data[i] == x

    def insert(self, x):
        i = self._bisect(x)
        if i < len(self.data) and self.data[i] == x:
            return  # already present — sets have no duplicates
        self.data.insert(i, x)

    def remove(self, x):
        i = self._bisect(x)
        if i < len(self.data) and self.data[i] == x:
            self.data.pop(i)

    def union(self, other):
        """O(n + m) merge of two sorted arrays."""
        result = OrderedSet()
        i, j = 0, 0
        while i < len(self.data) and j < len(other.data):
            if self.data[i] < other.data[j]:
                result.data.append(self.data[i])
                i += 1
            elif self.data[i] > other.data[j]:
                result.data.append(other.data[j])
                j += 1
            else:
                result.data.append(self.data[i])
                i += 1
                j += 1
        result.data.extend(self.data[i:])
        result.data.extend(other.data[j:])
        return result

    def intersection(self, other):
        result = OrderedSet()
        i, j = 0, 0
        while i < len(self.data) and j < len(other.data):
            if self.data[i] < other.data[j]:
                i += 1
            elif self.data[i] > other.data[j]:
                j += 1
            else:
                result.data.append(self.data[i])
                i += 1
                j += 1
        return result

    def difference(self, other):
        result = OrderedSet()
        i, j = 0, 0
        while i < len(self.data) and j < len(other.data):
            if self.data[i] < other.data[j]:
                result.data.append(self.data[i])
                i += 1
            elif self.data[i] > other.data[j]:
                j += 1
            else:
                i += 1
                j += 1
        result.data.extend(self.data[i:])
        return result

    def is_subset(self, other):
        return len(self.difference(other).data) == 0

    def __len__(self):
        return len(self.data)

    def to_list(self):
        return list(self.data)

# Test set axioms
A = OrderedSet()
for x in [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6]:
    A.insert(x)
assert A.to_list() == [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9]  # no duplicates, sorted

B = OrderedSet()
for x in [2, 7, 1, 8, 2, 8]:
    B.insert(x)
assert B.to_list() == [1, 2, 7, 8]

# Set operations
assert A.union(B).to_list() == [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]
assert A.intersection(B).to_list() == [1, 2]
assert A.difference(B).to_list() == [3, 4, 5, 6, 9]
assert B.difference(A).to_list() == [7, 8]

# Verify set-theoretic identities
# |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| (inclusion-exclusion)
assert len(A.union(B)) == len(A) + len(B) - len(A.intersection(B))

# A \ B = A ∩ B^c, so (A \ B) ∪ (A ∩ B) = A
recombined = A.difference(B).union(A.intersection(B))
assert recombined.to_list() == A.to_list()

The sorted array implementation makes the connection between mathematical set operations and algorithmic merge operations transparent. Union is merge. Intersection is merge-with-filter. The O(n + m) complexity comes directly from the merge pattern. A hash-based set gives O(1) expected membership but O(n + m) union — the same union cost, because you must visit every element regardless. The mathematical requirement (process all elements) determines the algorithmic lower bound.
